1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's the greatest freight transportation system in the world.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
A complex network woven into the very fabric of the United States.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:18,000
From the early iron horses to today's sleek steel stallions,

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
they grind up hillsides, snake through valleys,

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
thunder across prairies to deliver the goods.

6
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,080
Now, Freak Trains on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:44,380 --> 00:00:49,160
Welcome to North Platte, Nebraska, home of the largest rail yard in the world.

8
00:00:51,420 --> 00:00:53,720
Union Pacific's Bailey Yard.

9
00:00:53,721 --> 00:00:57,720
The yard is approximately 3,500 acres.

10
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:00,720
It's 8 miles long, about 2 miles wide.

11
00:01:00,721 --> 00:01:02,720
No other rail facility like in the world.

12
00:01:02,721 --> 00:01:05,720
Rail yards are the hubs of railroad operations.

13
00:01:05,721 --> 00:01:10,720
Here, freight cars from across the system are funneled in, sorted,

14
00:01:10,721 --> 00:01:15,720
and then reassigned to new trains that will bring them closer to their final destination.

15
00:01:15,721 --> 00:01:19,720
Just about anything that's produced in the United States is shipped by rail.

16
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:30,252
So if you're talking about wine or cheese, fresh vegetables, televisions, radios, automobiles, automobile parts, you name it, just about everything that America uses

17
00:01:30,253 --> 00:01:33,060
on a daily basis comes through North Platte.

18
00:01:36,360 --> 00:01:41,000
The Bailey Yard is strategically located in the center of the Union Pacific Rail network.

19
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:47,360
The place is so large, you can't see more than a third of it from any point in the yard.

20
00:01:49,720 --> 00:01:58,220
Well, that is, except from the computer screens of the command center, where all rail activity

21
00:01:58,221 --> 00:02:03,020
on the yard is monitored.

22
00:02:03,021 --> 00:02:08,400
What we do in the BCC, Bailey Command Center, is coordinating the movement of between 130

23
00:02:08,401 --> 00:02:11,600
and 180 trains per day.

24
00:02:11,601 --> 00:02:16,480
Utilizing the latest computer-based controls, the center monitors all traffic over Bailey

25
00:02:16,481 --> 00:02:23,800
yards 315 miles of track. In addition to locomotive movements, workers process

26
00:02:23,801 --> 00:02:29,020
10,000 freight cars through the yard each day. For some of these cars, their first

27
00:02:29,021 --> 00:02:34,060
stop is one of the two classification yards or hump yards. This is where we

28
00:02:34,061 --> 00:02:38,620
process eastbound trains. Trains that come into North Platte are going to be

29
00:02:38,621 --> 00:02:44,680
sorted here to their four destinations. So we are taking these cars apart that

30
00:02:44,681 --> 00:02:49,040
that have come in on a train, and then we're sorting them to go out destinations further

31
00:02:49,041 --> 00:02:50,860
points from here.

32
00:02:50,861 --> 00:02:56,400
The hump is a three-story man-made hill that allows up to four cars a minute to roll into

33
00:02:56,401 --> 00:02:58,400
its bowl.

34
00:02:58,401 --> 00:03:00,700
Gravity pulls them off the hill.

35
00:03:00,701 --> 00:03:05,880
The computer picks up the weight of the car as it goes over the scale rails here.

36
00:03:05,881 --> 00:03:11,760
The car then passes through a system of computerized retarders, or brakes, that pinch the wheels

37
00:03:11,761 --> 00:03:13,680
as they go through to slow the car down.

38
00:03:14,460 --> 00:03:16,800
It's pretty amazing that the amount of tonnage

39
00:03:16,801 --> 00:03:17,780
that's going over this hill,

40
00:03:17,980 --> 00:03:20,160
how softly it's handled down here

41
00:03:20,161 --> 00:03:21,680
when it hits the bottom end of things.

42
00:03:23,140 --> 00:03:25,960
The destination of each car is entered into a computer

43
00:03:25,961 --> 00:03:29,760
that automatically switches the track

44
00:03:29,761 --> 00:03:32,680
so the car rolls directly onto its assigned rail.

45
00:03:34,280 --> 00:03:37,560
At the trim tower at the other end of the ball are trim crews,

46
00:03:37,700 --> 00:03:39,900
and what they do is they actually build the trains

47
00:03:39,901 --> 00:03:41,300
that are going to leave North Platte.

48
00:03:41,301 --> 00:03:44,580
So we take them apart here, they put them back together down there.

49
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:47,740
Remote-controlled switch engines,

50
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:51,960
less powerful locomotives used for switching cars in a rail yard,

51
00:03:52,940 --> 00:03:57,700
assemble the trains when enough freight cars going to a single location have been humped.

52
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:02,520
The hump used to be the focal point of the railroad yard.

53
00:04:02,900 --> 00:04:05,500
Pretty much all traffic was handled in this fashion.

54
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:09,380
What's changed that has taken the pressure off of this

55
00:04:09,381 --> 00:04:11,520
are trains like you see leaving over there.

56
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:15,140
They leave their originating point all in one big chunk.

57
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:17,600
They arrive at their destination in one big chunk.

58
00:04:18,080 --> 00:04:21,760
Unit freight, or an entire train consisting of the same type of freight,

59
00:04:22,160 --> 00:04:24,880
like a coal train, is treated differently

60
00:04:24,881 --> 00:04:27,680
since it doesn't need to be reassigned.

61
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:30,760
For unit freight, there are 26 run-through tracks,

62
00:04:30,761 --> 00:04:32,080
some with pad areas,

63
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:35,280
which allow trains to pull up, refuel, and depart

64
00:04:35,281 --> 00:04:38,100
without having to detach from the locomotive.

65
00:04:38,101 --> 00:04:45,100
Every train that comes in must have a mechanical inspection by our car forces.

66
00:04:45,101 --> 00:04:49,100
We have a NASCAR pit team concept for our locomotives.

67
00:04:49,101 --> 00:04:55,100
They're a team of five men and women that attack each locomotive that comes in where they had to be fueled,

68
00:04:55,101 --> 00:04:59,100
the oil has to be checked, they shine the windshields, they get the locomotive ready to go.

69
00:04:59,101 --> 00:05:03,100
Our goal is to do it in 45 minutes to service the train completely.

70
00:05:03,101 --> 00:05:09,140
Cleveland and then we expect the train crew to be here as soon as we drop our protection,

71
00:05:09,141 --> 00:05:11,600
which is blue flags.

72
00:05:11,601 --> 00:05:16,840
In addition to these pit stops, the Bailey Yard boasts a locomotive repair depot the

73
00:05:16,841 --> 00:05:18,920
size of five football fields.

74
00:05:18,921 --> 00:05:22,520
This is not a heavy duty repair facility.

75
00:05:22,521 --> 00:05:28,860
What we do here is quarterly maintenance, semi-annual, annual and tri-annual standard

76
00:05:28,861 --> 00:05:31,860
maintenance on our locomotives.

77
00:05:31,861 --> 00:05:38,860
And just as importantly, rail cars are serviced at the Bailey Yard as well.

78
00:05:38,861 --> 00:05:48,860
Part of the issue we have with Dramas on occasion are our wheels or axles failing en route.

79
00:05:48,861 --> 00:05:55,860
And so our car department employees really focus in on the conditions of wheels and axles as they're coming through our run-through facilities.

80
00:05:55,861 --> 00:06:07,860
On a daily basis, we're averaging between 55 and 60 wheel sets changed out a day here at North Platte.

81
00:06:07,861 --> 00:06:11,860
Inspect over 13,000 freight cars a day through this facility.

82
00:06:11,861 --> 00:06:21,860
The Bailey Yard is home to 4,000 Union Pacific employees who work around the clock 365 days a year to keep freight moving.

83
00:06:21,861 --> 00:06:29,620
There's an absolute production mandate at Norplat, whether it's rain, snow, sun, blizzard,

84
00:06:29,621 --> 00:06:35,100
high winds, we must hit those production targets because if we don't, we will back up the railroad

85
00:06:35,101 --> 00:06:36,180
on both sides.

86
00:06:36,181 --> 00:06:40,100
And we cannot have that kind of impact on the Union Pacific system.

87
00:06:40,101 --> 00:06:48,100
We now return to freight trains on Modern Marvels.

88
00:06:48,101 --> 00:06:51,420
The Baileyard may be the largest rail yard.

89
00:06:51,421 --> 00:06:54,720
servicing the largest railroad in North America,

90
00:06:54,721 --> 00:07:00,020
but it's also a mammoth cog in a much larger machine.

91
00:07:00,021 --> 00:07:04,520
We're talking about a $40 billion a year industry,

92
00:07:04,521 --> 00:07:08,020
where 96 cents of every dollar made by the railroads

93
00:07:08,021 --> 00:07:10,020
comes from freight.

94
00:07:10,021 --> 00:07:16,520
A system so large, it encompasses over 140,000 miles of track

95
00:07:16,521 --> 00:07:20,520
and extends to nearly every corner of the continental United States.

96
00:07:20,521 --> 00:07:22,420
It's more than that.

97
00:07:22,421 --> 00:07:27,700
It's 22,000 locomotives and 1.3 million freight cars.

98
00:07:27,701 --> 00:07:33,080
It's trains 100 cars long, extending over a mile and a half.

99
00:07:33,081 --> 00:07:38,740
It's 1.8 billion tons of freight crossing the country each year.

100
00:07:38,741 --> 00:07:43,080
And it was the movement of freight that was the driving force behind the creation of the

101
00:07:43,081 --> 00:07:49,860
railroad.

102
00:07:49,861 --> 00:07:55,060
The railroad business was born in the form of rudimentary horse-drawn carts, like these.

103
00:07:55,280 --> 00:07:59,580
The whole principle is that you have steel wheels on steel rails,

104
00:07:59,820 --> 00:08:02,460
and that lowers the coefficient of friction.

105
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:06,240
You could pull more with less power on a steel rail

106
00:08:06,241 --> 00:08:10,680
than if you were trying to pull a wagon over the mud or dirt roads.

107
00:08:11,840 --> 00:08:15,700
Railways that utilized horses to move public goods soon emerged.

108
00:08:15,701 --> 00:08:27,763
After 1830, with the Tom Fum and the first successful steam engines, the best friend of Charleston, and those early ones, they proved that steam was reliable and more

109
00:08:27,764 --> 00:08:29,580
powerful than horsepower.

110
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:35,720
And it didn't take long for railroad entrepreneurs to set their sights on capturing the American freight business.

111
00:08:37,320 --> 00:08:40,680
A market cornered by the growing East Coast canals.

112
00:08:40,681 --> 00:08:46,400
Just about the time it was taking them to build the canals to a standard where they would be really useful.

113
00:08:46,700 --> 00:08:53,040
You could build a railroad next to the canal and just beat the pants off the canal boats with a train.

114
00:08:53,340 --> 00:08:57,680
And the railroad simply technologically replaced the canals almost overnight.

115
00:09:00,400 --> 00:09:03,200
Laborers built tracks as fast as humanly possible.

116
00:09:04,200 --> 00:09:08,860
By the 1850s, railroad tracks extended from the East Coast to the Great Lakes.

117
00:09:08,861 --> 00:09:19,660
In 1869, the Union Pacific and Central Pacific Railroads met at Promontory Summit, Utah, and completed the Transcontinental Railroad.

118
00:09:21,540 --> 00:09:27,520
The railroad business expanded very rapidly because you could make a lot of money at it.

119
00:09:28,020 --> 00:09:33,480
Railroads changed the landscape of America, creating new towns along their tracks,

120
00:09:34,480 --> 00:09:38,160
supplying the new population with building materials and other necessities,

121
00:09:38,860 --> 00:09:41,980
and carrying far-flung crops to markets back east.

122
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:47,620
They could send their money and their gold and stuff back from California to the bankers in the east

123
00:09:47,621 --> 00:09:50,000
so they could sit there on Wall Street and count their money.

124
00:09:50,720 --> 00:09:52,700
This was all because of the railroads.

125
00:09:53,280 --> 00:09:59,540
And people out west could get fancy French fashions from Broadway in New York

126
00:09:59,541 --> 00:10:02,460
and have it delivered to Market Street in San Francisco.

127
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:07,280
All of the things that people wanted and needed could be brought in by train.

128
00:10:07,281 --> 00:10:14,700
Into the 20th century, railroads continued to expand and develop faster than any other industry in the country.

129
00:10:15,440 --> 00:10:21,080
What separates the United States from the rest of the world is that the American railroads were all private enterprise.

130
00:10:21,640 --> 00:10:29,600
They were all private companies, highly competitive companies, often run by robber barons who would have financial wars and the whole business.

131
00:10:29,601 --> 00:10:39,900
Due to the infighting among the rival railroads, no cohesive system existed to move freight efficiently across the various independently owned lines.

132
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:47,040
During the First World War, there was concern that the railroads wouldn't be able to keep up with the demand for service.

133
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:53,180
And the federal government took over the operation of the railroads under the United States Railway Administration.

134
00:10:53,181 --> 00:11:00,180
Their idea was to standardize railroad operating procedures and try to make the railroads more efficient.

135
00:11:00,181 --> 00:11:06,180
Soon after the war, the government returned the rails to private enterprise.

136
00:11:06,181 --> 00:11:13,180
Twenty years later, by World War II, the collection of independent railroads operated as a coordinated system,

137
00:11:13,181 --> 00:11:16,180
and no government intervention was needed.

138
00:11:18,180 --> 00:11:22,180
The steel backbone of the nation played its part in winning the war,

139
00:11:22,181 --> 00:11:28,060
But by the 1950s, the railroad's monopoly on freight transportation was quickly eroding.

140
00:11:29,120 --> 00:11:34,280
Trucking and the newly created interstate highway system threatened to derail the railroads.

141
00:11:35,280 --> 00:11:40,980
Then the railroads, which were a network of little feudal domains of different railroads,

142
00:11:41,060 --> 00:11:45,760
all competing with each other in a system that was set up to compete among each other,

143
00:11:46,180 --> 00:11:49,860
were suddenly having to compete with a new outside competitor.

144
00:11:49,861 --> 00:11:56,860
Railroads responded by merging, as well as upgrading and modernizing.

145
00:11:56,861 --> 00:12:02,860
And today, freight trains are still the number one way to move goods long distances.

146
00:12:02,861 --> 00:12:09,860
But to keep themselves on top, their locomotives would need a 21st century overhaul.

147
00:12:09,861 --> 00:12:17,860
In 2004, there were 59 different freight lines operating in Pennsylvania, the most railroads in any of the United States.

148
00:12:17,861 --> 00:12:22,589
United States. Freight trains will return on Modern Marvels.

149
00:12:22,590 --> 00:12:24,480
We now return to Freight

