449
00:36:38,960 --> 00:36:43,420
They share some of the same rail, and they even use the same line in opposing directions.

450
00:36:44,560 --> 00:36:46,920
It may sound like a train wreck just waiting to happen.

451
00:36:46,921 --> 00:36:58,640
But every move of every train is closely monitored and calculated by dispatchers located at operations

452
00:36:58,641 --> 00:37:01,680
centers throughout the United States.

453
00:37:01,681 --> 00:37:06,080
The train crew has a responsibility to operate the train, but the train dispatcher gives them

454
00:37:06,081 --> 00:37:08,520
the route to run on.

455
00:37:08,521 --> 00:37:15,240
Train dispatchers are the rulers of the rails, and no move can be made without their authorization.

456
00:37:15,241 --> 00:37:25,240
VNSF Railway's Network Operations Center is command central for the dispatchers of one of the largest railroads in North America.

457
00:37:25,241 --> 00:37:30,240
The facility is 45,000 square feet, about the size of a football field.

458
00:37:30,241 --> 00:37:33,240
We house at any given time about 225 employees.

459
00:37:33,241 --> 00:37:37,240
There's 94 different dispatching workstations.

460
00:37:37,241 --> 00:37:45,760
Each dispatcher's territory is 200 to 500 miles, and their planning horizon is two to

461
00:37:45,761 --> 00:37:47,700
three hours ahead.

462
00:37:47,701 --> 00:37:52,680
He or she has a screen with a track layout on the computer screen, and it's all point

463
00:37:52,681 --> 00:37:53,680
and click.

464
00:37:53,681 --> 00:37:59,440
The dispatcher can point to a switch or a route and direct that train's movement over

465
00:37:59,441 --> 00:38:03,360
that with just a point and click operation.

466
00:38:03,361 --> 00:38:09,400
This way, it's the dispatcher that selects the path of the train, not the engineer.

467
00:38:09,401 --> 00:38:13,820
So it's imperative that the dispatchers know exactly where their trains are at all times.

468
00:38:13,821 --> 00:38:16,560
We've got two different tracking mechanisms.

469
00:38:16,561 --> 00:38:20,380
One's through the signal system that actually tracks trains through electric current in

470
00:38:20,381 --> 00:38:24,600
the rail that brings the information into the train dispatcher's dispatching system.

471
00:38:24,601 --> 00:38:30,760
And then secondly, we've got a wireless AEI, automatic equipment identification, that wirelessly

472
00:38:30,761 --> 00:38:35,800
transmits information into the train dispatcher and gives him or her location and route.

473
00:38:36,520 --> 00:38:42,420
And this is basically an RFID that we place on all the rail cars, all the locomotives,

474
00:38:42,740 --> 00:38:45,200
on every rail car in North America.

475
00:38:46,560 --> 00:38:51,660
BNSF has around 500 AEI readers, and as the trains go by these readers,

476
00:38:52,160 --> 00:38:57,700
it energizes the tag and captures what car that is or what locomotive that is,

477
00:38:57,701 --> 00:39:00,840
and then we use that information to update our mainframe

478
00:39:00,841 --> 00:39:04,780
and identify exactly what's on the train at that point in time.

479
00:39:06,160 --> 00:39:09,080
Within a few seconds of a train going by a reader,

480
00:39:09,560 --> 00:39:13,320
the information is available to the dispatchers at the network operations center

481
00:39:13,321 --> 00:39:15,380
as well as to customers online.

482
00:39:16,640 --> 00:39:19,660
But since readers may be up to 100 miles apart

483
00:39:19,661 --> 00:39:23,240
and conductors set out cars at various points along the route,

484
00:39:24,080 --> 00:39:26,420
dispatchers need even more accurate reporting.

485
00:39:26,421 --> 00:39:33,420
BNSF on a daily basis has somewhere around 1200 trains running at any point in time and

486
00:39:33,421 --> 00:39:38,300
basically if they aren't going by a reader they're reporting a lot of their information

487
00:39:38,301 --> 00:39:41,360
through the Voice Train Reporting application.

488
00:39:41,361 --> 00:39:45,380
The application automatically converts verbal information into computer data.

489
00:39:45,381 --> 00:39:50,740
So they basically just use the radio that they have, they tell us what they did, within

490
00:39:50,741 --> 00:39:54,840
nine seconds that information is updated in our mainframe.

491
00:39:54,841 --> 00:39:59,220
information can be anything from picking up cars to leaving cars out at various

492
00:39:59,221 --> 00:40:04,780
sidetracks and yards along the route. Handheld computers are also used to

493
00:40:04,781 --> 00:40:11,160
report car movement. This would be very similar to what UPS does in that that's

494
00:40:11,161 --> 00:40:14,960
how they keep track of their packages. This is how we keep track of our rail

495
00:40:14,961 --> 00:40:19,900
cars and if you consider a rail car is just a lot bigger package this is

496
00:40:19,901 --> 00:40:25,720
basically where we track where we left the car when we left it there and it's

497
00:40:25,721 --> 00:40:30,140
how we identify to the conductor you need to go pick up that package.

498
00:40:30,141 --> 00:40:34,900
Railroads are now exploring innovative ways to utilize Wi-Fi and GPS

499
00:40:34,901 --> 00:40:39,960
technologies to increase rail safety. An example of a safety initiative is

500
00:40:39,961 --> 00:40:45,640
electronic train management system or ETMS as we call it. ETMS is a virtual

501
00:40:45,641 --> 00:40:50,500
safety net for the train and has the ability to independently slow down or

502
00:40:50,501 --> 00:40:55,680
even stop a train electronically. The locomotive on board system would have the

503
00:40:55,681 --> 00:41:00,460
authority that's granted to the train, the speed, the distance that they have

504
00:41:00,461 --> 00:41:04,900
to travel with the authority that's been issued. The system automatically takes

505
00:41:04,901 --> 00:41:08,440
control of the train if the engineer exceeds the authority given to him by

506
00:41:08,441 --> 00:41:13,140
the dispatcher, fails to acknowledge a signal or even if he's about to take a

507
00:41:13,141 --> 00:41:18,900
curve too quickly. The system constantly monitors information about the path ahead

508
00:41:18,901 --> 00:41:23,580
of the train and determines what actions are required from the engineer. It gives

509
00:41:23,581 --> 00:41:27,420
a warning to the engineer if he's not taking the appropriate action to stop or

510
00:41:27,421 --> 00:41:33,300
slow down the train. If they violate that or don't react to stop the train before

511
00:41:33,301 --> 00:41:37,020
they get to the end of their authority, it'll automatically stop the train.

512
00:41:37,021 --> 00:41:42,880
Once implemented, ETMS will allow railroads to run trains more safely and

513
00:41:42,881 --> 00:41:43,765
efficiently.

514
00:41:43,766 --> 00:41:49,740
So as we move deeper into the 21st century, we can be sure that the rail industry

515
00:41:49,741 --> 00:41:54,280
will continue to be the best and most cost-effective way to move freight.

